Changes for 2.0-0 (2026-11-22)
  * borm/predict.borm: fixed a real bug exposed by rms::contrast.rms()
    (see below): its internal predict() call passes the data frame
    POSITIONALLY (predict(fit, d, type='x')), not as newdata=, which
    bormPredictYdep()'s plain dots$newdata lookup did not recognize,
    causing a gendata() crash for any yde-fitted model used with
    contrast(). Fixed with a new bormGetNewdata() helper that
    recognizes a positionally-passed data frame the same way
    rms::predictrms() itself does.
  * rms::contrast.rms (R/contrast.s, in the rms package): its
    ycut-passing logic was hardcoded to inherits(fit,'blrm'), so
    contrast() on a yde-fitted borm() model silently evaluated the
    Y-dependent effect at the reference intercept regardless of any
    ycut supplied, while still mislabeling the output as if ycut had
    been honored -- confirmed directly (not just reasoned through):
    contrast()'s value was identical with and without an explicit
    ycut, and differed from the correct value computed via
    predict(..., type='lp', ycut=...). Fixed by generalizing the
    check to also recognize a borm() fit with qyde>0, while keeping
    the separate cppo-requirement check blrm-specific (borm() has no
    cppo at all, it validates yde/ytrans/tau itself). Re-verified
    blrm()'s own existing cppo-based contrast() behavior is unchanged
    (inst/tests/blrm-cppo.r, blrm-contrast.r both re-run, identical
    values). A yde-fitted borm() fit now requires ycut for contrast(),
    matching blrm()'s own partial-PO requirement, and the returned
    value now changes correctly with ycut, matching predict()'s own
    ground truth exactly.
  * borm-yde.r: extended with tests for both of the above.
  * borm: implements ydep via new arguments yde (a formula, paralleling
    blrm()'s own ppo) and ytrans (a function, paralleling cppo but
    generalized to return a matrix of one or more columns instead of a
    single score) -- a single-column ytrans applied to a single yde
    predictor reduces mathematically to blrm()'s cppo mechanism
    exactly, confirmed via direct comparison under real NUTS sampling
    on identical data (blrm() needs normcppo=TRUE to match ytrans's
    own unconditional auto-centering/scaling). A genuinely multi-
    column (m>1) Y-dependent effect was validated on simulated data
    with a known, strongly Y-dependent true treatment effect: the
    yde-fitted model's loo elpd was far better than the plain
    proportional-odds model's (-779 vs -1253 in the test), confirming
    real signal recovery rather than merely running without error.
  * ormcon.stan: added q/m/Z/ytransmat/tausd data and a q x m tau
    parameter matrix, replacing the old always-zero lp2 placeholder.
    ZT = Z*tau and the per-observation Y-dependent contributions at
    the ia/ia2 alpha indices are computed inside Stan (transformed
    parameters), not in R, since they combine a parameter with data --
    the same reasoning already documented for lp = X*beta. The Y-
    level-specific row of ytransmat is gathered via a +1 shift from
    alpha-index to Y-level-index (ia_p1/ia2_safe_p1, precomputed once
    in transformed data), exactly paralleling blrm's own
    pposcore[ia+1]/pposcore[ia2+1] convention; confirmed this +1
    shift, Stan's matrix-row-gather-by-integer-array syntax, and
    rows_dot_product() all give numerically correct results via a
    standalone test harness before relying on them in the real model.
    tau gets a single scalar-SD normal(0, tausd) prior on every entry
    by default (unlike blrm's own constrained-PPO tau, which has no
    default prior at all) -- Frank's explicit choice for borm. q=0/
    m=0 (yde absent) make Z, ytransmat, and tau all zero-sized;
    confirmed via a standalone test harness that this compiles and
    gives an identically-zero contribution, not an error. The new
    ydeterm1/ydeterm2 terms are added into each pw_log_lik_* family's
    e1/e2_raw before the existing has2/sign-selection dummy-pair
    substitution, so the NaN-safety already built for those hazards
    (see the 1.2-0 entry below) covers the new term for free.
  * borm: ytrans's result is automatically centered and scaled
    (independently per column, using the EMPIRICAL distribution of
    observed Y -- ytrans(ylev[Y[,1]]) -- not a uniform average over
    distinct levels, exactly matching blrm's own cppo-centering
    convention under normcppo=TRUE) before being used; this is
    unconditional for borm (no opt-out), per Frank's explicit
    instruction. tau is back-transformed from the QR-transformed Z
    scale to the original covariate scale (bormYdepBT()), and the
    corresponding alpha/intercept adjustment implied by Z's own
    centering is computed per alpha index (varies by Y-level, unlike
    blrm's single constrained-PPO tau's uniform-across-levels
    adjustment) -- confirmed self-consistent via a direct check that
    reconstructing the linear predictor from the back-transformed
    alpha/beta/tau at a covariate's original scale exactly reproduces
    the raw QR-scale linear predictor Stan actually optimized.
  * borm: yde predictors may (and usually will) overlap formula's own
    predictors, per Frank's explicit instruction, exactly like ppo's
    existing semantics.
  * predict.borm: for a yde-fitted model, implements its own
    yde-aware type='lp' and type='x' (the two most commonly needed,
    and the building blocks type='fitted' etc. use internally),
    generalizing predict.blrm()'s hardcoded single-column cppo logic
    to tau's q x m matrix via bormPredictYdep(). Other types, and
    fun=, are not yet implemented for a yde-fitted model and raise an
    informative error rather than silently ignoring the Y-dependent
    effect. Building Z at predict time needed two fixes to how
    rms::Design() is called, neither about yde logic itself, both
    confirmed directly with standalone reproductions: (1) a newdata
    frame containing columns beyond yde's own formula (e.g. the main
    formula's own covariates) made rms::Design() fail once more than
    one row was requested, apparently via a datadist interaction --
    fixed by subsetting to yde's own variables first; (2) rms::Design()
    cannot build contrasts for a categorical predictor from newdata
    containing only one of its levels, unlike predictrms()'s own
    machinery for the main formula -- fixed by releveling factor/
    character columns to the full level set seen when yde was fit.
    Also fixed, found via the same direct verification: kint must be
    re-derived from a user-supplied ycut (predict.blrm() does this;
    an early version of bormPredictYdep() did not, so the alpha part
    of lp silently used the default reference intercept regardless of
    ycut while the tau part correctly used it) -- caught by a manual
    cross-check of predict()'s output against the fit's own stored
    coefficients, not by inspection.
  * Mean.borm/Quantile.borm/ExProb.borm: now error informatively
    rather than silently ignoring yde/ytrans when delegating to their
    blrm() counterparts (which are hardcoded to a single-column cppo);
    not yet implemented for a yde-fitted model.
  * borm: npcontrast now implemented, as a combined beta+tau contrast
    mechanism (bormDoconYdep(), ormcon.stan's new cn2/C2/cmus2/csds2
    data and normal_lpdf(C2 * append_row(beta, to_vector(tau)) |
    cmus2, csds2) prior term) -- turned out to need more than the
    originally-planned generalization of rms::Xcontrast() (Path 1):
    beta and tau are mechanically separate Stan parameter blocks, so
    a single-design-matrix contrast row (as docon() builds for
    pcontrast, or as blrm()'s own single-column-tau npcontrast uses)
    cannot express a quantity like "the effect of a predictor at a
    given Y value", which is beta_j + Z_j . tau . ytransmat[y,] --
    necessarily mixing both. Each named predictor-setting list in
    npcontrast may carry an optional ycut (a single Y value applied
    to every setting, or a named vector giving a different Y value
    per setting, exactly paralleling blrm's existing ycut
    convention); a setting with no ycut contributes a pure-beta row.
    No change to rms::Xcontrast() itself was needed after all: each
    setting's row is built by calling it twice (once against the
    main formula, once against yde) and combining in R, which turned
    out simpler and more general than modifying the shared rms
    function. Confirmed directly (not just reasoned through, in
    inst/tests/borm-yde.r): a pure-beta npcontrast (no ycut) gives
    exactly the same beta-part as an equivalent pcontrast, with its
    tau-part exactly 0; two settings sharing the same covariate
    values but different ycut have their beta-parts cancel to
    exactly 0 in a difference, giving a pure-tau contrast; a mixed
    contrast populates both parts, with the beta-part identical to
    the pure-beta case (confirming it is ycut-independent, as the
    math requires) -- i.e. simple contrasts automatically drop the
    unneeded part, with no special syntax needed for any of the
    three cases. Finding and fixing this also exposed a real,
    pre-existing, unrelated bug: docon() returns the QR-transformed
    contrast under the name C, not Contrastt, so fit.object$Contrastt/
    $nContrastt have always silently been NULL for both blrm() and
    borm() (the actual Stan-side C/C2 data were unaffected, since
    those were already built from the correct field) -- fixed in both
    blrm.r and borm.r.
  * borm-yde.r: new test in inst/tests covering all of the above,
    including the full npcontrast mechanism.
  * Stan: use built-in log_inv_logit_diff for stable interior log-likelihood
  * Accuracy: replace log(inv_logit(ca) - inv_logit(ca1)) with Stan's
    built-in log_inv_logit_diff(ca, ca1) at every uncensored-interior and
    interval-censored log-likelihood term in lrmconppo.stan,
    lrmconppot.stan, and lrmcppo.stan (pw_log_lik and Pr). Avoids
    catastrophic cancellation when both cutpoints are deep in the same
    tail (large |eta| or many intercepts); naive formula returned NaN on
    14% of a 198-point test grid and up to 2.3% relative error where
    finite, vs ~1e-15 (machine epsilon) for the built-in, measured against
    an arbitrary-precision (Decimal) reference. Verified blrm(optimizing)
    matches rms::orm MLEs and -2LL on simulated data (k=4 and k=9
    categories, with and without cppo), and a short NUTS run completes
    without error.
  * Cleanup: remove the three commented-out log_diff_exp/log1p_exp dead-
    code alternatives in lrmcppo.stan that had anticipated this fix but
    were never wired in.
  * blrm: Stan code (lrmconppo.stan, lrmconppot.stan, lrmcppo.stan) now
    takes precomputed per-observation intercept subscripts (ia, ia2,
    sgn) as data, from the new rms::ormIdx() wrapper around the same
    Fortran ormidx routine rms::orm.fit() already uses, instead of the
    raw censored y. Removes every censoring-type conditional from the
    Stan likelihood; the Stan code is otherwise unchanged and no score/
    Hessian is computed, matching the design rms::orm.fit() already
    uses and that the future rmsb::borm() will reuse.
  * blrm: fixes two bugs this exposed/removed as a side effect of the
    above. (1) Censoring: blrm's own Ncens counts and the data it sent
    to Stan assumed a stale 1..k integer-only y convention that no
    longer matches Ocens2ord()'s actual -Inf/Inf censoring sentinels,
    so censored fits were silently broken; blrm now converts to the
    exact -1/k+1 sentinel convention rms::orm.fit() uses before calling
    ormIdx(). (2) cppo: the old lrmconppo.stan/lrmconppot.stan
    left-censoring branch applied a frozen pposcore[2] non-PO score
    regardless of the censoring bound, instead of varying with it like
    every other branch; the new unified ia/ia2-indexed formula applies
    pposcore[ia+1] uniformly, eliminating that branch-specific bug by
    construction.
  * blrm: the unconstrained PPO model (ppo without cppo) still does not
    support censored Y, but now fails with an informative error instead
    of silently corrupting the response via as.integer(-Inf) = NA.
  * DESCRIPTION: bumped required rms version to >= 8.2-2, which exports
    ormIdx().
  * blrm/print.blrm: fit object now stores Ncens1 and Ncens2 (original
    vs. post-coding censoring counts), taken directly from Ocens2ord()'s
    own attributes, matching rms::orm.fit() exactly, in place of a single
    Ncens field that silently matched Ncens2 and could disagree with
    orm's printed count whenever a right-censored point at/beyond the
    highest uncensored value was reclassified to uncensored at a new
    tail category. print.blrm now displays Ncens1, matching print.orm().
    This replaces the fit object's Ncens field -- not backward compatible
    with fits saved before this change.
  * ormcon.stan: new Stan code for the borm function (see below);
    implements all 5 rms::orm() link functions (logistic, probit,
    loglog, cloglog, cauchit), numerically accurate calculations, and
    both the single-sigma and rms::orm.rfit() dual-sigma (mix_re)
    random-intercept extensions.
  * ormcon.stan: vectorized the logistic/loglog/cloglog likelihoods
    (no per-observation loop at all; probit/cauchit remain scalar
    loops, since their numerically-stable forms -- std_normal_lcdf/
    cauchy_lcdf -- exist only as Stan's summing distribution functions,
    with no accuracy-preserving elementwise alternative). Found and
    fixed three distinct 0 .* non-finite = NaN hazards this
    "compute both branches, blend by multiplying by 0/1" style of
    vectorization introduced, confirmed by direct reproduction in each
    case, not just reasoned through: (1) the two-component (has2)
    blend's dummy index satisfied log_inv_logit_diff/log_diff_exp's
    x > y ordering requirement but not finiteness -- fixed by feeding a
    FIXED dummy pair (0, -1), not one derived from the real intercept
    value. (2) loglog/cloglog's sign (CDF-vs-CCDF) selection could
    overflow the unused branch at ordinary, non-extreme eta (as low as
    ~8-9 for cloglog, since its CDF saturates doubly-exponentially
    fast) -- fixed the same way, feeding 0 to the unused branch. (3) A
    deeper version of the same issue was an actual SEGFAULT/bus error
    (not just NaN) in logCDF_loglog/logCCDF_cloglog's raw exp() calls
    overflowing during Stan's reverse-mode autodiff, reproduced in a
    1-line standalone model and fixed by capping the argument to exp()
    at 700 (comfortably below the ~709.78 double-precision overflow
    point), the same defensive principle Stan's own log1p_exp/log1m_exp
    already use. All three fixes verified via a full re-run of the
    5-link rms::orm comparison suite (unaffected) plus targeted
    regression tests reproducing each original failure.
  * borm: new function, the Bayesian analog of rms::orm() the way
    blrm() is the Bayesian analog of rms::lrm() -- all 5 rms::orm()
    link families, exact censoring handling via rms::ormIdx() (as
    blrm() now also uses), a Dirichlet-only intercept prior (no
    alternative priors, per simulations showing they slow sampling
    badly once the number of distinct Y values is large -- the regime
    borm() is meant for), pcontrast (reusing blrm()'s exact mechanism;
    npcontrast is accepted as an argument for forward compatibility but
    errors if supplied, since there is no non-proportional-odds
    submodel to apply it to until ydep is implemented), and both the
    single-sigma and rms::orm.rfit() dual-sigma (mix_re) random-
    intercept extensions -- mix_re is a mix_re() formula special used
    together with cluster(), exactly as rms::orm() itself handles it,
    not a borm() argument. As with blrm(), covariates are centered and
    QR-orthogonalized in R before being passed to Stan; the Stan code
    itself has no knowledge of either transformation.
  * borm: implements Spearman's rho and Somers' Dxy following
    rms::orm()'s own censoring rules exactly (rho is NA whenever any
    censoring is present; Dxy/C are NA only when left- or interval-
    censoring is present, using rms::Ocens2Surv()/survival::
    concordancefit() for the right-censoring-only case), each with a
    highest posterior density interval computed the same way blrm()
    already computes one for its other predictive-accuracy measures --
    see the new bormStats()/print.bormStats(), paralleling
    blrmStats()/print.blrmStats() but generalized to the actual link
    family (rms::probabilityFamilies) instead of a hardcoded plogis().
  * borm: print.borm() gives a family-dependent title and subtitle
    matching rms::print.orm()'s own family-dependent title switch
    (prefixed "Bayesian", as blrm()'s titles are) and blrm()'s
    always-Dirichlet subtitle. predict.borm()/Mean.borm()/
    Quantile.borm()/ExProb.borm() delegate entirely to the existing
    blrm() versions, which were already link-and-model-structure-
    agnostic (dispatching via object$link/rms::probabilityFamilies,
    with their only ppo/cppo-specific logic guarded behind fields
    borm() always sets to 0/NULL) -- confirmed by inspection before
    reusing rather than duplicating ~400 lines of logic.
  * stanMisc.r: extracted docon() (the pcontrast/npcontrast design-
    matrix-and-prior builder) out of a closure inside blrm() into a
    shared top-level function, now used by both blrm() and borm(), per
    this package's own standing rule that a helper needed in more than
    one file must not live inside another function's body.
  * ormcon.stan: added pcontrast support (cn/C/cmus/csds data fields
    and the corresponding normal_lpdf prior term), matching blrm()'s
    Stan files, and added a gamma transformed parameter (the per-
    cluster random intercept, scaled by sigmag for the single-sigma
    case or left as the standardized v_i -- matching
    rms::orm.rfit()'s own reporting convention -- when mix_re is
    active) that blrm()'s existing draws-extraction convention
    requires by that exact name; its absence was caught by testing
    (every clustered borm() fit failed with "no parameter gamma" before
    this fix), not anticipated in advance.
  * Build: wired ormcon.stan into the package build via
    rstantools::rstan_config() (regenerates R/stanmodels.R and
    src/stanExports_ormcon.*/RcppExports.cpp automatically from the
    .stan files present in inst/stan/ -- no file needed hand-editing).
  * Verified borm() against rms::orm() MLEs (method='optimizing'):
    matches closely for logistic (uncensored and with left/right/
    interval censoring mixed) and probit; confirmed cluster() and
    cluster()+mix_re() sample cleanly under real NUTS; confirmed
    pcontrast actually constrains beta (a tight prior pulls it to
    ~0.0006 vs. ~0.76 under a near-flat one) rather than being silently
    ignored; confirmed npcontrast's intended error. R CMD check status:
    OK (two environment-only INFO notes: the mice suggested package
    being unavailable to check with, and GNU make being a
    SystemRequirements -- neither related to this work).
  * borm-blrm.r, borm-orm.r: new tests in inst/tests
  * blrm/npcontrast: `ycut` was documented and accepted (see the
    `pcontrast=pcon(0.3), ycut=1` example already in
    inst/tests/blrm-pcontrast.r) but silently discarded by the shared
    docon() helper, so users had to manually pre-scale `sd` by
    `cppo(ycut)`'s value themselves (per npcontrast's own doc text).
    Fixed: docon() now applies `cppo(ycut)` to each named setting's
    Z-row automatically for npcontrast (a no-op for pcontrast, since
    its Stan-side prior multiplies C against beta alone and a
    cppo-weighted row would not match beta's dimension). `ycut` may
    also now be a named vector giving a different Y cutoff per named
    setting within one npcontrast specification -- e.g. to put a prior
    directly on the difference between an effect evaluated at two
    different Y values. Backward compatible: only activates when
    `ycut` is explicitly supplied, which previously had no effect at
    all. Verified the auto-weighted raw contrast exactly matches the
    old manual-prescaling workaround, that omitting `ycut` reproduces
    identical (unweighted) behavior, that a named two-Y-value `ycut`
    gives the expected `cppo(y2)-cppo(y1)` weighted difference, and
    that pcontrast's own `ycut` handling is unchanged (re-ran
    inst/tests/blrm-pcontrast.r). R CMD check status: OK.

Changes for 1.1-3 (2026-10-29)
   * blrm: added sampling.args to hash digest
   * print.blrm: changed to use new version of prModFit in rms
   * print.rmsb: fixed old bug related to rownames being lost with drop=TRUE which surfaced with one predictor and intercepts=FALSE
   * cluster: removed this function as it is now in the new, required rms package

Changes for 1.1-2 (2025-04-13)
   * predict.blrm: fixed type='fitted' for binary Y; thanks Christian Hametner: <https://discourse.datamethods.org/t/bayesian-regression-modeling-strategies/6105/99>
   * removed Ocens function which is now in rms 7.0
   * changed to be compatible with storage of functions in rms::probabilityFamilies
   * updated to use Ocens2ord on top of Ocens, needs rms 8.0-0

Changes for 1.1-1 (2024-07-07)
   * blrm: added argument showopt to show Stan optimizer steps, default is FALSE
   * blrm: added options(rmsbmsg=FALSE) to allow the user to suppress information messages
   * lrmconppot.stan: fixed bug for dim(C2) p -> q
   * replaced use of survival::survConcordance and Hmisc::somers2 with concordancefit

Changes for 1.1-0 (2024-03-11)
   * Store cppo as deparsed for opt object from optimization (MLE) to prevent huge environment from being carried along
   * blrm: Non-downward compatible changes: removed keepsep argument
   * blrm: Added npcontrast argument for specification of priors for non-proportional-odds parameters in the constrained partial PO model through the use of contrasts, parallel to pcontrast for the PO part of the model; changed 3 stan code modules for this

Changes for 1.0-0 (2023-09-26)
   * blrm: added support for cmdstan and cmdstanr package using backend=
   * When using cmdstan uses bayesplot package for pairs plots
   * stanDx: new code for cmdstan diagnostics
   * stackMI: corrected erroneous implementation of file= where it was only applying to one of the imputations; also handled generally the conversion of functions in fit object to character strings for efficient serializing
   * Added non-exported function cluster so as to not depend on survival package
   * blrm: added sampling.args argument
   * blrm: trapped ppairs error in case plot region too small to hold graph
   * blrm: added pcontrast argument for user-specified priors on linear combinations of regression coefficients; required changing all Stan code
   * blrm: removed priorsd argument (NON-DOWNWARD-COMPATIBLE CHANGE)

Changes for 0.1-0 (2022-04-12)
   * blrm: got around bug in model.response not keeping class Ocens
   * soprobMarkovOrdPost: removed since Hmisc has soprobMarkovOrdm
   * lrmconppot.stan: removed redundant pi simplex (thanks Mark Jones <mark.jones1@sydney.edu.au>

Changes for 0.0-2 (2021-02-27)
   * Removed @title lines from Roxygen comments
   * Enhanced predict.blrm and changed prediction methods to use it
   * Changed storage of cppo function in fit object to character strings so as to not have saveRDS serialize it with all its environment data
   * Added lrmconppot.stan to implement flat and t-distribution priors for intercepts
   * blrm: added iprior argument for lrmconppot
   * predict.blrm: fixed fun= with finint=FALSE
